course: dictionary
lesson: 11978
title: Word parts and clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some long words are built from smaller parts you may already know. Look for a part you recognise, then use it to guess the meaning. This is another way of using context and clues. «Cent» is a part you may know from maths and science, and it means one hundred.
explain: Centenarian is built from «cent», meaning 100, and an ending that means a person. A centenarian is a person who is 100 years old or older. «My great-grandma became a centenarian last spring, so we had a big party.» The clues are the party and the number 100 hidden inside the word.
explain: Other words with «cent» work the same way. A century is 100 years. A centimetre is one hundredth of a metre. When you meet a new long word, check its parts first, then test your meaning in the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
fact: Centenarian is a noun: a centenarian, centenarians.
fact: «Centenarian» means a person who is 100 years old or older.
fact: «Cent» is a word part meaning one hundred, as in century and centimetre.
fact: People often celebrate when a grandparent becomes a centenarian.
fact: The phrase is "become a centenarian", as in «She became a centenarian on her birthday.»
exercise: «My gran became a centenarian on Sunday.» What is a centenarian? | *A person who is 100 years old or older | A special birthday cake | A young child who has just started school | A very old tree in the park
exercise: Which word uses the same part as centenarian, meaning 100? | Dozen | Decade | Pair | *Centimetre
exercise: «The class made a card for the centenarian at the care home.» Find and copy the word that names the person. | card | class | home | *centenarian
test: «Mrs Okafor celebrated her 100th birthday, and now she is a centenarian.» What does centenarian mean? | A person aged 10 | A kind of party game | *A person aged 100 or more | A birthday card
test: Which word is built from «cent», meaning 100, like centenarian? | Pair | Dozen | *Century | Decade
exam: Jayden says «centenarian» is a hard word to read. What is the best way to work out what it means? | Ask someone to read it louder | Skip it and never read the sentence again | *Look at the part cent, meaning 100, and check the sentence clues | Guess that it is a type of food
